SUMMARY: 9 of 10 criteria PASS. Criterion 7 (comments and docs match the code)
FAILS on two stale/overbroad documentation claims about the guard's reach. The
guard itself is sound: I could not construct any Makefile target or plain
`go test` invocation that runs this package's tests and silently skips the
harness. The defect is documentation-only and is two sentences wide.

=====================================================================
(1) THE ORACLE -- PASS
=====================================================================
  $ bash .swarm/tier3/S5/accept.sh ; echo $?
    == S5 oracle ==
    PASS  go build ./...
    PASS  go vet ./...
    PASS  go test ./...
    PASS  harness runs and passes when node is present
          opt-out variable: BUDGET2_ALLOW_SKIP_JS
    PASS  node absent and no opt-out -> test fails loudly
    PASS  the failure message names node
    PASS  opt-out set -> skips cleanly with a named reason
    PASS  make test-unit fails when node is absent (exit 2)
    PASS  the Makefile target-scanning machinery is gone
    PASS  non-test targets (build, clean, vet, dev) do not require node
    PASS  a harness reporting FAIL while exiting 0 fails the Go test
    ORACLE: PASS
    0
Every property below was then established independently of accept.sh, by
running the commands myself against a PATH with node's directory removed.

JUDGEMENT ON THE REWRITTEN ORACLE (asked for explicitly). Deleting the
Makefile-scanning checks did NOT drop coverage the run still needs. The old
checks tried to prove a property over an unbounded space ("every recipe that
could ever invoke go test carries the prerequisite") using literal patterns,
which is why they shared the evasion blind spot. The new design makes the
property structural -- the test function itself refuses -- so it is provable
once, per invocation path, rather than enumerated over Make idioms. Nothing
the old checks could see is now unseen: I re-ran the full target set by hand
(item 4 below) and all of it fails.
Three real weaknesses remain in accept.sh, none of them fatal, all worth
recording because the oracle is the contract:
  a. The opt-out variable is DISCOVERED by regex
     (`[A-Z][A-Z0-9_]*(SKIP|ALLOW)[A-Z0-9_]*` | sort -u | head -1) rather than
     named. It resolves correctly today (BUDGET2_ALLOW_SKIP_JS), but a future
     token sorting earlier would silently redirect checks (a) and (b) at the
     wrong variable and still print ORACLE: PASS.
  b. It asserts only that the failure message "names node". Criterion 2 also
     requires the harness and the opt-out route to be named; the oracle cannot
     see that. I checked it directly (item 2).
  c. It spot-checks only `make test-unit`. The other four invocation paths
     criterion 4 names are unverified by the oracle. I ran all five (item 4).
  d. Nothing in the oracle inspects comments or docs, which is exactly where
     this attempt fails (item 7). The oracle cannot be the whole gate here.

=====================================================================
(2) NODE ABSENT, NO OPT-OUT -> HARD FAILURE -- PASS
=====================================================================
PATH built by deleting only node's own bin dir:
  $ STRIPPED=$(printf '%s' "$PATH" | tr ':' '\n' \
      | grep -v -F "$(dirname "$(command -v node)")" | paste -sd: -)
  $ PATH="$STRIPPED" command -v node   -> (empty)  # node genuinely gone

  $ PATH="$STRIPPED" go test -count=1 ./internal/handlers/accounts/
    FAIL	budget2/internal/handlers/accounts	0.214s   (exit 1)
Not a skip: `grep -c -- '--- SKIP'` over the verbose run returns 0 for this
test, and the failure line is warnings_client_regression_test.go:141.
The message (verbatim from make-test-unit output line 169) contains all three
required elements:
  - names node:        "`node` was not found on PATH"
  - names the harness: "(testdata/js/warnings_dom_harness.js)" and
                       "accounts.html's syncWarnings() script under node"
  - names the opt-out: "or set BUDGET2_ALLOW_SKIP_JS=1 to deliberately opt out
                        and get a clean skip instead of this failure"
It also states the policy in terms: "so this test FAILS rather than skips when
node is missing".
Sibling test TestExtractSyncWarningsScript_FindsExactlyOneBlock still PASSes
with node absent (make-test-unit line 167), so the node requirement is scoped
to the one test that needs it.

=====================================================================
(3) OPT-OUT SEMANTICS -- PASS (documented, defensible, consistent)
=====================================================================
The rule in code is os.Getenv("BUDGET2_ALLOW_SKIP_JS") != "" -- presence, i.e.
ANY non-empty value opts out. Measured, node absent:
  $ PATH=$STRIPPED BUDGET2_ALLOW_SKIP_JS= go test ...      -> FAIL   (empty = not set)
  $ PATH=$STRIPPED BUDGET2_ALLOW_SKIP_JS=0 go test -v ...  -> --- SKIP / ok
  $ PATH=$STRIPPED BUDGET2_ALLOW_SKIP_JS=false go test ... -> ok
So `=0` and `=false` DO opt out. That is the trap the brief warned about, but
it is not silent and not undocumented: README.md:643-646 says "set
BUDGET2_ALLOW_SKIP_JS to any non-empty value", the test's doc comment says "to
any non-empty value" twice, and the failure message a user sees before ever
setting it says "set BUDGET2_ALLOW_SKIP_JS=1". The rule stated is exactly the
rule implemented. I accept it: it is the conventional Go presence check, the
documentation is unambiguous, and the empty-string case (the one a shell most
easily produces by accident, e.g. an unset variable expanded into the env)
correctly does NOT opt out.
The skip is clean and named:
  --- SKIP: TestSyncWarnings_ClientRegressionHarness
      "skipping client-side warnings regression harness: `node` was not found
       on PATH, and BUDGET2_ALLOW_SKIP_JS is set, so this is a deliberate
       opt-out rather than an accidental one..." -> package exits ok / 0.
Additional property I checked that nobody asked for and that matters: the
opt-out CANNOT silence a machine that has node. The env check sits inside the
exec.LookPath error branch, so
  $ BUDGET2_ALLOW_SKIP_JS=1 go test -count=1 -v -run TestSyncWarnings... 
    --- PASS: TestSyncWarnings_ClientRegressionHarness (0.02s)
i.e. a CI box with node installed still RUNS the harness even with the opt-out
exported globally. That closes the obvious "set it in CI once and forget"
evasion.

=====================================================================
(4) THE GUARD TRAVELS -- PASS
=====================================================================
All five invocation paths, node absent, each run to completion:
  make test-unit      exit 2
  make race           exit 2
  make test-coverage  exit 2
  make test           exit 2
  go test ./...       exit 1
  make check          exit 2   (vet static vuln test)
Each fails through the TEST, not a Makefile prerequisite. Proof per run:
  $ grep -niE 'node is required|check-node|Error: node|node not found' <output>
    -> no match in any of the five
  $ grep -n 'was not found on PATH' <output>
    -> warnings_client_regression_test.go:141 in all five
  followed by FAIL budget2/internal/handlers/accounts.
Serious attempt to route around it, all with node absent:
  - go test -short ./internal/handlers/accounts/         -> FAIL (no Short() gate)
  - go test WITHOUT -count=1 after a green cached run    -> FAIL (cache does not
    mask it; exec.LookPath's PATH read participates in the test cache key)
  - build tags: the test file carries no //go:build line; `grep -n go:build
    internal/handlers/accounts/*.go` -> none. No -tags value excludes it.
  - TestMain: `grep -rn "func TestMain" internal/handlers/accounts/` -> none,
    so there is no package-level hook that could turn the Fatal into a skip.
  - a FAKE node (shim that exits 0 printing nothing) early on PATH -> FAIL
    (the Go glue requires three `^RESULT <name> PASS` lines, not exit status).
    So "install a stub node" is not an escape either; it fails closed.
The only paths I found that do not fail are explicit test filters:
  - go test -run TestExtractSyncWarningsScript ./internal/handlers/accounts/ -> ok
  - GOFLAGS='-run=^$' go test ./internal/handlers/accounts/ -> ok [no tests to run]
These do not run the harness test at all; a -run filter that excludes a named
test is an unremovable property of `go test` itself, not of this design, and no
Makefile target uses one. I do not count them against criterion 4, but I record
them so nobody claims a stronger property than exists.
Two targets DO run `go test` and exit 0 with node absent -- `make
test-integration` (`go test -v ./cmd/server/...`, exit 0) and `make fuzz`
(no fuzz packages, exit 0). Neither compiles internal/handlers/accounts, so
neither skips the harness; they are not criterion-4 violations. They ARE the
counterexample to a sentence in the test's doc comment -- see item 7.

