(6) THE HARNESS STILL CATCHES THE REAL REGRESSION -- PASS
=====================================================================
Done entirely outside the repo. I copied the tree (excluding .git, binaries,
data) to
  .../scratchpad/s5/defectcopy
and reintroduced the point-16 early return in the COPY's accounts.html only,
by moving the banner lookup to the top of syncWarnings():
    function syncWarnings() {
        var data = document.getElementById('accounts-warnings-data');
        if (!data) return;
        var banner = document.getElementById('accounts-warnings-banner');
        if (!banner) return;          <-- the S4/S5 defect, restored
        var text = ...
Result in the copy:
  $ go test -count=1 -v -run TestSyncWarnings_ClientRegressionHarness ./internal/handlers/accounts/
    RESULT dismiss_resolve_recreate FAIL {"postDismissBannerAbsent":true,
      "postDismissStored":true,"bannerPresent":false,"dismissPresent":false,
      "regionAssertsWarning":false,"parityOK":true,
      "regionText":"Pattern overlap warning dismissed.", "dataText":"..."}
    RESULT dismiss_then_reload_unchanged PASS {...}
    RESULT guard_directions FAIL {"unrelatedWrites":0,"changedWrites":1,
      "clearingEmpty":false}
    --- FAIL: TestSyncWarnings_ClientRegressionHarness (0.02s)   exit 1
Two of the three named checks go red on the exact defect this task exists to
prevent. The repo's own template was never touched: its md5 is
163d70d7849ce363f1c463052805db32 before and after.
I also read the harness rather than trusting its check names. They assert what
they claim: testDismissResolveRecreate actually dispatches the dismiss click,
verifies the dismissal took effect (banner removed AND sessionStorage key
stored) before proceeding, then drives resolve and recreate through
renderSwap() with fresh DOM nodes and a fired htmx:afterSwap, and asserts the
parity invariant rather than string equality of copy.
testDismissThenReloadUnchanged builds a genuinely new DOM twice while carrying
sessionStorage across, which is what a reload is. testGuardDirections counts
actual textContent writes to the region (instrumented in the setter), so
"unrelatedWrites === 0 / changedWrites === 1" is a real measurement, not a
proxy. The Go glue requires `^RESULT <name> PASS(\s|$)` for all three names,
so a harness that crashes mid-way (the catch block emits the FUNCTION name,
e.g. testDismissResolveRecreate, not the check name) also fails closed.

=====================================================================
(7) COMMENTS AND DOCS MATCH THE CODE -- **FAIL**
=====================================================================
The README paragraph (README.md:637-648) is accurate: "requires node on PATH
and fails the build (it does not skip) if node is missing, so this check cannot
be silently routed around by running go test directly, make test-unit, an IDE
test runner, or CI... set BUDGET2_ALLOW_SKIP_JS to any non-empty value". Every
clause of that is demonstrated in items 2-4.
The Makefile comment (Makefile:155-167) is accurate, including its closing
line "No target below requires node -- the test does."
Two other claims are not accurate.

7a. STALE POINTER TO DELETED MACHINERY -- the harness header, which is in the
S5.5 manifest, still credits the deleted Makefile guard:
  internal/handlers/accounts/testdata/js/warnings_dom_harness.js:14-16
    // `node` binary. See internal/handlers/accounts/warnings_client_regression_test.go
    // for how it is wired into `go test`, and the Makefile `test` target for
    // why a machine without node cannot silently skip it in CI.
The Makefile `test` target is now `test: check-go` + `$(GO) test ./...` and
nothing else; the comment directly above it says the opposite of what this
sentence promises -- "That guard lives in the test itself, not here". A reader
following the reference lands on a target that provides no such guarantee.
This is the same defect class the criterion names: a comment attributing
protection to a place the code does not deliver it. Attempt 5 rewrote the test
comment and the Makefile comment for the new design and left this one behind.

7b. OVERBROAD CLAIM IN THE TEST'S PACKAGE COMMENT --
  internal/handlers/accounts/warnings_client_regression_test.go, package
  comment, final sentence:
    "The Makefile carries no node-checking machinery of its own any more;
     `make test`, `make test-unit`, `make race`, and every other target that
     runs `go test` fails through this test's own t.Fatal, not through a
     Makefile prerequisite."
Falsified, node absent:
  $ PATH="$STRIPPED" make test-integration ; echo $?
    --- PASS: TestMCPEndpointRejectsSpoofedHostOnLoopbackConnection (0.02s)
    ok  	budget2/cmd/server	4.742s
    0
  $ PATH="$STRIPPED" make fuzz ; echo $?
    No fuzz tests found. ...
    0
`test-integration` runs `$(GO) test -v ./cmd/server/...` -- it is a target that
runs `go test`, and it does not fail. `fuzz` likewise. The sentence names three
targets correctly and then universally quantifies over a set it does not hold
for. The guarantee that IS true and that the design delivers is narrower:
every target that runs THIS PACKAGE'S tests fails through this test's t.Fatal.
I acknowledge a generous reading exists (that the clause describes the
mechanism of failure rather than asserting universal failure), but the sentence
carries no scoping qualifier, and this run has twice paid for comments whose
generous reading was true and whose literal reading was not. A reader who
believes the literal sentence concludes that a green `make test-integration`
implies node was present.

Both are documentation-only; neither weakens the guard. Fix is two sentences:
scope 7b to the tests of this package, and repoint 7a at the test file. I am
recording FAIL rather than nit because the criterion is explicit that an
overclaim here is a FAIL, and because "the comment says the Makefile protects
you" is precisely the belief that made attempts 2 and 4 look finished.

