=====================================================================
PROCESS: THE DECLARED TIER-3 DEVIATION
=====================================================================
Asked for explicitly, so recorded either way. The lead's reasoning -- that the
user's 2026-08-20f directive removed the design ambiguity N-version exists to
explore -- is HALF right, and I do not fully accept it.
Accepted: the directive did fix the design. There was no space left to search,
and a second blind arm would have produced a near-identical implementation.
Not accepted: CLAUDE.md's own Tier-3 text says that with both arms on Claude,
N-version "catches slips and misreadings rather than shared blind spots". Slips
and misreadings survive a fixed design -- and the only defects I found in this
attempt (item 7) are exactly that class: two sentences left describing the old
design. A comparison run would not reliably have caught them either (accept.sh
inspects no comments, so both arms could diverge on comment text without any
check firing), so I do not claim the deviation caused the failure. What I do
claim is that the stated reason -- "no ambiguity left" -- addresses only the
blind-spot rationale and not the slip-catching one that the constitution says
is the operative benefit here.
One mechanical consequence the lead should see before running the gate:
.swarm/tier3/S5/report.md still carries the attempt-3 RESOLUTION line
("RESOLUTION: merge wt-primary as-is; graft nothing from wt-alt...", written
16:50, before accept.sh was rewritten at 17:34). Per CLAUDE.md, `gate.sh check`
at Tier 3 requires "both the RESOLUTION line and a PASS from each lane at that
attempt". The RESOLUTION requirement will therefore be satisfied at attempt 5
by an artifact that adjudicates a different attempt's arms. That is a gate the
deviation walks through rather than trips; whether it should be re-stated for
attempt 5 is the lead's call, not mine.

=====================================================================
WHAT WOULD MAKE THIS PASS
=====================================================================
Nothing behavioural. Two comment edits:
 1. warnings_dom_harness.js:14-16 -- stop citing "the Makefile `test` target"
    as the reason node cannot be silently skipped; the reason is the test's own
    t.Fatal in warnings_client_regression_test.go.
 2. warnings_client_regression_test.go package comment, final sentence --
    scope "every other target that runs `go test`" to targets that run this
    package's tests, or drop the universal and keep the three named targets
    (make test / test-unit / race, all verified true).
Everything else in this attempt is verified correct.
